{
  "slots": {
    "question": [
      {
        "claim": "Does the political climate where someone lives change the relationship between their own political orientation and their COVID-19 vaccine hesitancy and uptake? Previous studies had largely examined individual orientation and local climate separately.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "While previous research has identified the unique impact\nof both political orientation and political climate on vaccination attitudes, there has\nbeen little research on the interaction between personal political orientation and local\npolitical climate.",
            "page": 2
          },
          {
            "text": "The present research examines political climate as a potential moderator of the\nassociation between political orientation and COVID-19 vaccine hesitancy and\nuptake.",
            "page": 2
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Does the pattern differ between initial vaccination and boosters, and between attitudes toward a dose and reported receipt of it?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Additionally, by looking at both COVID vaccine and booster status and hesitancy, we\nidentify how attitudes and behaviors may have shifted as we transitioned from initial vaccine doses to boosters.",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "central_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "Regional political climate was more strongly associated with hesitancy and booster uptake among conservatives than among liberals. Conservatives in more liberal areas were less hesitant and more likely to report getting a booster than conservatives in more conservative areas. This interaction did not extend to receipt of the initial vaccine.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "our findings indicate that political climate, as represented through regional 2020 presidential election votes,\nhas a stronger association with vaccine and booster hesitancy for individuals who are higher on political conservatism as\ncompared to those who are politically liberal.",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "This same trend was observed for self-reported booster status, but not self-­\nreported vaccine status.",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "By looking at political\norientation and region climate, we identify a novel and important finding, specifically that conservative individuals are more\nlikely to received booster doses in liberal regions.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "evidence": [
      {
        "claim": "The study used a self-report survey administered online to a U.S. nonprobability convenience sample recruited through Qualtrics Panels from March 8 to April 19, 2023.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "This was an online self-reported study in which a nonprobability convenience sample\nwas recruited from the United States between March 8 and April 19, 2023.",
            "page": 2
          },
          {
            "text": "Participants were a convenience sample recruited through Qualtrics Panels, an online research platform that manages\nparticipant recruitment and data quality for academic studies.",
            "page": 3
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "This was a new analysis of data originally collected to study racial and socioeconomic marginalization in relation to COVID-19 vaccine attitudes and behaviors.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The data used in this study were part of a larger project assessing the impact of racial and socioeconomic marginalization on COVID-19 vaccine attitudes and behaviors [27]. The present research presents a novel analysis of the\ndataset from this project, focusing on political orientation and political climate, rather than marginalization.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Recruitment used four race-by-income quotas. The targets were equal numbers of Black-only and White-only participants, half below $70,000 in annual income within each racial group, and no gender accounting for more than 60% of any quota group.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "the sample was recruited across four quota groups based on race and\nincome. Half of the sample was requested to be White (and no other racial identity), and half the sample was requested\nto be Black (and no other racial identity).",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "Within each racial group, half of the participants were requested to be below\nthe median annual income in the US [$70,000 [28]].",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "Additionally, within each quota group it was requested that no more than 60% of the\nparticipants be of any gender.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The analysis retained 683 of 1,777 respondents after excluding 970 who failed Qualtrics quality screens, nine whose open-ended answers did not make sense, and 115 without valid ZIP codes or corresponding election data. The authors required respondents to pass both panel screening and their own attention checks.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "To ensure data quality, we only include participants in our analysis that pass both\nQualtrics Panels data quality screening and our own attention checks (S1 File), as recommended by previous research\nusing Qualtrics Panels [26].",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "Qualtrics Panels collected data from 1777 participants, and identified 970 participants as not passing all quality screens\n(described above). We eliminated an additional nine participants whose open-ended responses did not make logical\nsense, resulting in a sample of 798 validated participants.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "We eliminated an additional 115 participants who did not provide valid zip code data (N = 100) or whose zip code did not have corresponding election data (N = 15). The final sample\nfor these analyses included 683 participants.",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Participants were 18 to 94 years old and were split almost exactly evenly between Black and White respondents. About 76% reported initial vaccination and 53% reported at least one booster.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Participants ranged in age from 18 to 94 years (M = 46.29, SD = 18.94). Demographic characteristics of the sample are\nshown in Table 1. 519 (76%) of participants reported receiving the initial COVID-19 vaccine and 365 (53%) reported\nreceiving at least one COVID-19 booster.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "Race\nBlack\n\n342 (50%)\n\nWhite\n\n341 (50%)",
            "page": 6
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The models accounted for age, gender, education, income, race, and perceived vaccine access. Perceived access was measured with one five-point item about how easily respondents could get a vaccine or booster if needed.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Perceived vaccine access was assessed using a single-item question “If I needed to get a COVID-19 vaccine\nor booster, I could easily get it”, rated on a 5-point Likert scale from Disagree Strongly (1) to Agree Strongly (5).",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "Note: Both models controlling for age, gender, income, education, race, and vaccine access.",
            "page": 9
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Individual conservatism was the average of two self-ratings, one on social issues and one on economic issues, each scored from 0 for strongly liberal to 10 for strongly conservative.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "asked participants to indicate their political orientation on\n“social issues (for example, abortion, gun rights, gay rights)”.",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "their political orientation on\n“economic issues (for example, taxation, government spending)”. Both items were rated on a 11-point sliding scale from\nstrongly liberal (0) to strongly conservative (10). These two items were averaged to form a political conservatism scale.",
            "page": 3
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Regional conservatism was measured as the percentage of votes cast for the Republican presidential candidate in 2020 in a participant’s ZIP-code area. The authors linked ZIP codes to election results from the New York Times using FIPS codes and averaged the data when more than one FIPS code matched.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We assessed regional political climate using zip code data provided\nby participants and 2020 presidential election results sourced from the New York Times [22].",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "Political climate for each participant was determined by matching FIPS codes to corresponding zip\ncodes. In cases where a zip code included multiple FIPS codes, data was averaged across these FIPS.",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "Using the FIPS\ndata for each zip code, we calculated the percent of Republican votes by dividing the number of Republican votes by the\ntotal number of votes (multiplied by 100).",
            "page": 3
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Initial vaccine and booster receipt were measured with separate self-report questions, each allowing yes, no, or don't know. A booster meant a dose given after the initial vaccine series.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Self-reported vaccine status and booster status were assessed through individual items, “have you received a COVID19 vaccine” and “have you received a COVID-19 booster”.",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "The term “booster” is used in this analysis as this was the\ncommon term at the time to refer to subsequent doses of the COVID-19 vaccine after the initial vaccine series. Possible\nresponses were yes, no, and I don’t know.",
            "page": 3
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The vaccine hesitancy score averaged seven five-point items adapted from the Attitudes towards Adult Vaccination Scale to refer specifically to COVID-19 vaccination. The booster hesitancy items came from a 2022 survey.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Vaccine hesitancy (i.e., negative attitudes toward vaccination) was measured\nusing the seven items from the Attitudes towards Adult Vaccination Scale [24], which assesses the perceived value of\nvaccines in general. We modified the items to assess attitudes specifically toward the COVID-19 vaccine",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "Each item was rated on a 5-point Likert scale ranging from disagree\nstrongly (1) to agree strongly (5). These items were averaged to form a vaccine hesitancy scale. Booster hesitancy was\nassessed using items from a 2022 survey on COVID-19 booster hesitancy [25].",
            "page": 3
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors used confirmatory factor analysis and assessed internal consistency with Cronbach’s alpha. Both hesitancy scales had an alpha of .93 and were scored by averaging their items.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We performed confirmatory factor analysis on the items for the vaccine and booster hesitancy scales (S2 File) and the\nsubsequent reliability of the scales was calculated using Cronbach’s alphas.",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "The vaccine hesitancy items demonstrated adequate reliability (α = .93, 95% CI [.92,.93]) and were\naveraged to form the vaccine hesitancy scale. The booster hesitancy items demonstrated adequate reliability (α = .93, 95%\nCI [.93,.94]) and were averaged to form the booster hesitancy scale.",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors used Pearson correlations for non-nominal variables, t-tests for mean comparisons, and chi-square tests for differences in vaccination and booster frequencies by gender and race.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Pearson correlations were used to assess\ncorrelations between all non-nominal variables (i.e., age, income, education, perceived vaccine access, vaccine and\nbooster hesitancy, political conservatism, and conservative regional climate).",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "differences in\nfrequencies of vaccine status and booster status by gender and race were assessed using X2. Independent samples\nt-tests were used to assess differences in all non-nominal variables across vaccinated and unvaccinated participants.\nThe same analyses were run to assess differences between boosted and unboosted participants.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors used ordinary least squares for the two hesitancy outcomes and logistic regression for the two uptake outcomes. They added individual conservatism and regional climate to the controls, then added their interaction to test moderation.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Multiple linear regression models were built to assess the association between political conservatism and conservative regional climate with\nvaccine hesitancy, controlling for age, gender, education, income, race, and perceived vaccine access. Ordinary least\nsquares estimation was used with default standard errors.",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "Model 2 included control variables and political conservatism and conservative regional climate. Model 3 included all variables from Model 2 and the interaction between\npolitical conservatism and conservative regional climate.",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "same process was used with booster hesitancy as the outcome variable to assess the association between political conservatism and conservative regional climate with booster hesitancy, controlling for age, gender, education,\nincome, race, and perceived vaccine access.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "Additionally, we ran two sets of generalized linear models with logit link\nand model-­based standard errors predicting vaccine status and booster status from the same three models described\nabove.",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Analyses excluded respondents with missing data on the relevant variables, used R 4.4.1, and treated two-sided p-values below .05 as statistically significant.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "For all analyses, any participants with missing data on any of these variables were excluded from the analysis.\nAll analysis and data visualization was conducted with R version 4.4.1 [29]. Two-sided p-values less than 0.05 were\nconsidered statistically significant.",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The study received ethics approval before data collection, and participants gave written informed consent through the online survey.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Ethical approval was obtained prior to data collection from the first author’s institutional review board (protocol ER-F22-35,\napproved on 1 October 2022) and all research was conducted in accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki. All participants completed an informed, written consent as part of the online survey.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "key_findings": [
      {
        "claim": "In the adjusted analyses, both individual conservatism and a more conservative regional climate were associated with higher initial-vaccine and booster hesitancy and lower reported uptake of both.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Political conservatism and conservative regional climate were both associated with higher levels of COVID-19 vaccine and booster hesitancy and lower rates of self-reported vaccination and boosting, when controlling for age, education, income, and race.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Initial-vaccine hesitancy was low among the most liberal participants regardless of regional climate. It rose with individual conservatism, and the rise was steeper in more conservative regions.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The interaction for vaccine hesitancy indicates that at the lowest levels of conservatism (i.e., liberalism) vaccine hesitancy is lower regardless of regional climate.\nAs political conservatism increases vaccine hesitancy also increases, but this increase is even greater for individuals\nwho live in a more conservative regional climate.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Conservatives in conservative regions were more hesitant about boosters than conservatives in liberal regions. Among the most liberal participants, booster hesitancy was slightly lower in more conservative regions.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "There was a similar pattern for booster hesitancy, with more politically\nconservative individuals in a conservative regional climate having higher booster hesitancy than conservative individuals\nin a liberal regional climate. For the most politically liberal individuals, booster hesitancy was slightly lower in more conservative regional climates.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The most liberal participants had an estimated probability of booster uptake above 65% regardless of climate. For the most conservative participants, it was about 60% in liberal regions and below 35% in conservative regions.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "the most liberal participants were over 65% likely to get boosted regardless of climate. The most conservative participants in liberal climates were 60% likely to get boosted, while conservatives in conservative climates were less\nthan 35% likely to get boosted (OR =.83, p = .026).",
            "page": 8
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "There was no statistically significant interaction between individual conservatism and regional climate for receipt of the initial vaccine. The authors suggest that widespread vaccine mandates may help explain why the interaction appeared for hesitancy but not for initial uptake.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The interaction between political conservatism\nand conservative regional climate (Table 6) was not significant (OR = 1.02, p = .833).",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "The interaction for vaccination was not significant, this\ndifference between attitudes (hesitancy) and action (vaccination), likely reflects the high prevalence of COVID-19 vaccine\nmandates.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "scope_and_limits": [
      {
        "claim": "Election returns are a proxy for political climate, not a way to isolate it from other local characteristics. Other ZIP-code differences, such as population density, could account for the associations.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Because we used 2020 presidential election votes by zip code as a proxy for political climate, it must be noted that\nother zip code-based differences could be driving the associations noted in this paper. Regional factors (e.g., population\ndensity) may play a role in linking 2020 election votes to vaccine attitudes and behaviors.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Adjusting for demographics did not establish that the associations were the same across demographic groups. The pooled estimates are sample averages, and subgroup differences could produce an aggregation effect such as Simpson’s paradox.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "While our analyses controlled\nfor demographic covariates commonly associated with vaccine hesitancy, we did not formally assess effect modification\nby these factors. Consequently, the reported pooled associations should be interpreted as average effects across the\nsample, and we cannot exclude the possibility that heterogeneity across demographic subgroups could produce aggregation effects such as Simpson’s paradox.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Reported vaccination status may not match actual status because self-reports can be affected by recall and social desirability bias.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Self-report is susceptible to social desirability [30] and recall bias [31], and we cannot assume that reported vaccine\nstatus reflects actual vaccine status.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Online recruitment excluded people without access to the platform, and the sample included only Black and White Qualtrics panelists. The findings may not generalize to the wider U.S. population.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Additionally, our sample was recruited through an online platform and only includes\nparticipants with access to this platform. Further, our sample includes only White and Black participants recruited through\nQualtrics Panels, and may not generalize to broader populations in the United States.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The cross-sectional design cannot establish causation or the direction of the associations.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Finally, it should be noted that this\ndata is cross-sectional and we cannot assume causal or directional associations.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The study did not test what explains higher booster uptake among conservatives in liberal areas. Social pressure is one possibility, and the study does not distinguish whether liberal environments exert more influence or conservatives are more responsive to their surroundings.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "While we did not asses the mechanism through which this occurs, these\nfindings suggest that environmental factors (e.g., social pressure) may impact decisions to receive annual COVID-19\ndoses.",
            "page": 10
          },
          {
            "text": "The interaction between political climate and\norientation suggests that liberal environments may exert more influence on conservative individuals than conservative\nenvironments exert on liberal individuals. It could also be the case that conservative individuals are more influenced by\ntheir environments than their liberal counterparts.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "theoretical_positioning": [
      {
        "claim": "The authors extend and combine two strands of vaccine-attitudes research: one focused on individual political orientation and the other on regional political climate. Rather than treating them only as separate predictors, the authors test whether their associations with vaccination attitudes and behavior depend on one another.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Previous studies have focused on either political orientation or political climate. By looking at political\norientation and region climate, we identify a novel and important finding, specifically that conservative individuals are more\nlikely to received booster doses in liberal regions.",
            "page": 10
          },
          {
            "text": "This interaction assessed the extent to which the association\nbetween political conservatism and vaccine attitudes/behavior was moderated by conservative regional climate.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "interlocutor_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "Rönn, Menzies, and Salomon found that counties and states with larger Democratic vote shares in the 2020 presidential election had higher COVID-19 vaccination rates in 2021–2022. Dinero and colleagues report a compatible association and build on it by testing whether the relationship with regional climate varies by a person’s own politics.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "For example, at both the county and the state level there was\na positive association between Democratic vote share (i.e., % of people voting for the\nDemocratic candidate) in the 2020 presidential election and COVID-19 vaccination\nrates from 2021−2022 [19].",
            "page": 2
          },
          {
            "text": "Our research is consistent with previous findings that conservative political orientation and a conservative\npolitical climate are both associated with COVID-19 vaccine hesitancy and decreased likelihood of receiving a COVID-19\nvaccine [6,7,10,19].",
            "page": 10
          },
          {
            "text": "The present research provides the first assessment of the potential interaction between political orientation, political\nclimate, and COVID vaccination.",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
